1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,541
Ensembles combine many trees.

2
00:00:02,541 --> 00:00:06,690
Bagging and random forests average deep trees to cut variance.

3
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:10,171
Boosting adds small trees one at a time to cut bias.

4
00:00:10,171 --> 00:00:14,253
The exam tests which is which, plus two buggy-code questions.

5
00:00:15,867 --> 00:00:18,440
Bagging, short for bootstrap aggregating,

6
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:23,149
draws B bootstrap samples from the training set, grows a deep tree on each,

7
00:00:23,149 --> 00:00:26,225
then averages their predictions, or takes a vote.

8
00:00:26,225 --> 00:00:28,924
Deep trees have low bias but high variance,

9
00:00:28,924 --> 00:00:31,749
and averaging many of them cuts the variance.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:38,026
So, true or false: the main purpose of bagging is to estimate out of sample error.

11
00:00:38,026 --> 00:00:38,429
False.

12
00:00:38,429 --> 00:00:40,041
It's to reduce variance.

13
00:00:40,041 --> 00:00:43,532
The out of bag error estimate is a handy by-product.

14
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:48,799
A random forest is bagging plus one twist: at each split,

15
00:00:48,799 --> 00:00:53,043
the tree may only consider a random subset of m features out of p.

16
00:00:53,043 --> 00:00:57,416
That stops every tree from using the same strong feature at the top,

17
00:00:57,416 --> 00:01:01,531
so the trees are less correlated, and averaging them helps more.

18
00:01:02,287 --> 00:01:07,529
Practice midterm one asks why random forest trees are less correlated: the

19
00:01:07,529 --> 00:01:11,072
bootstrap samples, and the random feature subsets.

20
00:01:11,072 --> 00:01:12,135
Both, answer C.

21
00:01:12,135 --> 00:01:15,748
And if m equals p, a random forest is just bagging.

22
00:01:17,333 --> 00:01:19,025
Boosting works differently.

23
00:01:19,025 --> 00:01:21,281
It builds small trees one at a time,

24
00:01:21,281 --> 00:01:25,793
and each new tree is fit to the errors the current ensemble still makes,

25
00:01:25,793 --> 00:01:28,363
then added in with a small learning rate.

26
00:01:28,363 --> 00:01:30,493
It learns slowly, which cuts bias.

27
00:01:31,253 --> 00:01:32,965
Three true or false facts.

28
00:01:32,965 --> 00:01:38,166
Boosting is inherently sequential, while random forests can train every tree in

29
00:01:38,166 --> 00:01:39,153
parallel: true.

30
00:01:39,153 --> 00:01:44,090
Boosting works because each tree is very large: false, it uses small trees.

31
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:49,620
And the number of splits per tree is a setting you tune with cross-validation: true.

32
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,491
Here's the comparison to memorize.

33
00:01:53,491 --> 00:01:58,073
Bagging and random forests train independent deep trees, in parallel,

34
00:01:58,073 --> 00:01:59,135
to cut variance.

35
00:01:59,135 --> 00:02:02,589
Boosting trains small trees in sequence to cut bias.

36
00:02:03,353 --> 00:02:08,610
The overfitting question: adding more trees to a random forest doesn't overfit; it

37
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:09,828
just averages more.

38
00:02:09,828 --> 00:02:12,329
But more boosting rounds eventually do,

39
00:02:12,329 --> 00:02:15,598
and so does adding quadratic terms to a regression.

40
00:02:15,598 --> 00:02:20,278
That's why the answer to practice midterm three, question thirteen, is B.

41
00:02:20,278 --> 00:02:25,022
And no method always wins: a linear model can beat a forest out of sample.

42
00:02:26,633 --> 00:02:31,407
Practice midterm two has a programming bug: bagging that samples without

43
00:02:31,407 --> 00:02:32,202
replacement.

44
00:02:32,202 --> 00:02:37,506
Drawing n rows from n rows without replacement just reshuffles the training set.

45
00:02:37,506 --> 00:02:41,749
So every tree is identical, and their average is that same tree.

46
00:02:41,749 --> 00:02:47,052
The bagged model performs exactly like a single CART tree with cp equal to zero.

47
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:51,823
Now a random forest with the same bug, with m smaller than p.

48
00:02:51,823 --> 00:02:57,007
The data never changes, but each split still draws a random subset of features,

49
00:02:57,007 --> 00:02:58,320
so the trees differ.

50
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,813
Averaging them still reduces variance.

51
00:03:00,813 --> 00:03:05,473
Expect performance at least as good as CART with cp zero in most cases,

52
00:03:05,473 --> 00:03:10,001
and often better, though less of a gain than a correct random forest.

53
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:12,130
To recap.

54
00:03:12,130 --> 00:03:16,488
Bagging averages deep trees grown on bootstrap samples, to lower variance.

55
00:03:16,488 --> 00:03:20,198
A random forest adds a random subset of features at each split,

56
00:03:20,198 --> 00:03:22,553
and with m equal to p it's just bagging.

57
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:26,675
Boosting adds small trees in sequence, each fit to the current errors.

58
00:03:26,675 --> 00:03:30,327
More forest trees don't overfit, but more boosting rounds can.

59
00:03:30,327 --> 00:03:33,212
And if the bootstrap samples without replacement,

60
00:03:33,212 --> 00:03:35,568
bagging collapses to a single CART tree.

61
00:03:35,568 --> 00:03:38,335
Next: the smaller topics, and an exam playbook.
